#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096518 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096726 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e2f40eb3-6087-5c38-b7c6-e160ff925bc6" :knowledge-point/title "Interpreting Linear Correlation Coefficients in Context"}] :question/id #uuid "aa7ce13a-2f5f-55f5-bac0-ced1a321d5b5" :question/math-academy-id "e-5711" :question/problem "A stall selling lemonade is set up at a special four-day summer festival. The stall owner wants to check the strength of the linear correlation between $x,$ the temperature at $12\\,\\textrm{p.m},$ and $y,$ the number of cups of lemonade sold that day. The data is shown below. \n\n| **Temperature at \n $12$ p.m. ($^\\circ \\mathrm{C}$)** | **$26$** | **$30$** | **$22$** | **$18$** |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| **Cups of Lemonade Sold** | $100$ | $118$ | $124$ | $86$ |\n\nYou are given that the means are $\\overline{x}=24$ and $\\overline{y}=107.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $\\rho\\approx 0.54$\n\nII. There is a strong positive correlation between $x$ and $y.$\n\nIII. There is a weak positive correlation between $x$ and $y.$\n\n*Hint: $|\\rho| > 0.7$ indicates a strong correlation, and $0.3 \\leq |\\rho| \\leq 0.7$ indicates a weak correlation.*" :question/worked-solution "The linear correlation coefficient $\\rho$ of the data sets $x=\\big\\{ x_1,x_2, \\ldots, x_n \\big\\}$ and $y=\\big\\{ y_1,y_2, \\ldots, y_n \\big\\}$ is given by \n\n$$\n\\rho(x,y) =  \\dfrac{\\textrm{Cov}(x,y)}{\\sigma_x \\cdot \\sigma_y} = \n\\dfrac{S_{xy}}{\\sqrt{S_{xx} \\cdot S_{yy}}}.\n$$\n\nIn addition, we have the following rule of thumb:\n\n- if $|\\rho| \\gt 0.7,$ the correlation is *strong*\n\n- if $0.3\\leq |\\rho| \\leq 0.7,$ the correlation is *weak*\n\n- if $|\\rho| \\lt 0.3,$ then the correlation is *negligible*\n\nLet's now examine our statements in turn:\n\n- Statement I is true. To calculate $\\rho,$ we first compute the sum in the numerator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}_{xy} & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})({y}_{i}-\\overset{―}{y}) \\\\  & =(26-24)(100-107)+(30-24)(118-107) \\\\  & +(22-24)(124-107)+(18-24)(86-107) \\\\  & =(2)(-7)+(6)(11)+(-2)(17)+(-6)(-21) \\\\  & =-14+66-34+126 \\\\  & =144\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSecond, we compute the sums in the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}_{xx} & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})^{2} \\\\  & =(26-24)^{2}+(30-24)^{2}+(22-24)^{2}+(18-24)^{2} \\\\  & =4+36+4+36 \\\\  & =80 \\\\ {S}_{yy} & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({y}_{i}-\\overset{―}{y})^{2} \\\\  & =(100-107)^{2}+(118-107)^{2}+(124-107)^{2}+(86-107)^{2} \\\\  & =49+121+289+441 \\\\  & =900\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we use the formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\rho (x,y) & =\\frac{144}{\\sqrt{80}\\cdot \\sqrt{900}} \\\\  & ≈\\frac{144}{8.944\\,272\\cdot 30} \\\\  & ≈0.54,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $2$ decimal places.\n\n- Statement II is false, while statement III is true. Since $0.3\\leq |\\rho| \\leq 0.7$ the correlation is weak. Also, since $\\rho$ is positive, the correlation is positive.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096522 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096727 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "76569338-1e2a-5b12-96ef-685d272343cd" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Correlation Coefficient Using Covariance and Standard Deviations"}] :question/id #uuid "5002af3e-d0f0-59eb-a52d-bfd7d101115e" :question/math-academy-id "e-5725" :question/problem "Consider the following data sets:\n\n$$\nx = \\big\\{ 6, \\: 3, \\: -1, \\: 0 \\big\\},\\qquad\ny = \\big\\{ 5, \\: 7, \\: -2, \\: 6 \\big\\}\n$$\n\nYou're given that $\\overline{x}=2,$ $\\overline{y}=4,$ $\\sigma_x \\approx 2.739,$ and $\\sigma_y \\approx 3.536.$ Calculate the corresponding linear correlation coefficient. Round your answer to $2$ decimal places." :question/worked-solution "The (population) linear correlation coefficient of the data sets $x=\\big\\{ x_1,x_2, \\ldots, x_n \\big\\}$ and $y=\\big\\{ y_1,y_2, \\ldots, y_n \\big\\}$ is given by \n\n$$\n\\rho(x,y) =  \\dfrac{\\textrm{Cov}(x,y)}{\\sigma_x \\cdot \\sigma_y}\n$$\n\nwhere $\\textrm{Cov}(x,y)$ is the covariance, and $\\sigma_x$ and $\\sigma_y$ are the standard deviations of the data sets.\n\nFirst, we need to compute the covariance:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Cov}(x,y) & =\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})({y}_{i}-\\overset{―}{y}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}[(6-2)(5-4)+(3-2)(7-4)+(-1-2)(-2-4)+(0-2)(6-4)] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}[(4)(1)+(1)(3)+(-3)(-6)+(-2)(2)] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}[4+3+18-4] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot 21 \\\\  & =5.25\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we use the formula. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\rho (x,y) & =\\frac{\\text{Cov}(x,y)}{{\\sigma }_{x}\\cdot {\\sigma }_{y}} \\\\  & ≈\\frac{5.25}{2.739\\cdot 3.536} \\\\  & ≈0.54\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $2$ decimal places."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096593 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096786 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "dd2caf12-80c2-553a-9737-64151565cf86" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Linear Correlation Coefficient Using the Expanded Formula"}] :question/id #uuid "8c2381d6-a9af-5ce3-aa20-d0e19a7a7229" :question/math-academy-id "e-9710" :question/problem "Consider the following data sets:\n\n$$\nx = \\big\\{ 13, \\: 10, \\: 9, \\: 12 \\big\\},\\qquad\ny = \\big\\{ -1, \\: 3, \\: 2, \\: 4 \\big\\}\n$$\n\nYou are given that $\\overline{x}=11$ and $\\overline{y}=2.$ Calculate the corresponding linear correlation coefficient. Round your answer to $2$ decimal places." :question/worked-solution "The linear correlation coefficient $\\rho$ of the data sets $x=\\big\\{ x_1,x_2, \\ldots, x_n \\big\\}$ and $y=\\big\\{ y_1,y_2, \\ldots, y_n \\big\\}$ is given by \n\n$$\n\\rho(x,y) =  \\dfrac{\\textrm{Cov}(x,y)}{\\sigma_x \\cdot \\sigma_y} = \\dfrac{S_{xy}}{\\sqrt{S_{xx} \\cdot S_{yy}}}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute the sum in the numerator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}_{xy} & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})({y}_{i}-\\overset{―}{y}) \\\\  & =(13-11)(-1-2)+(10-11)(3-2) \\\\  & +(9-11)(2-2)+(12-11)(4-2) \\\\  & =(2)(-3)+(-1)(1)+(-2)(0)+(1)(2) \\\\  & =-6-1+0+2 \\\\  & =-5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSecond, we compute the sums in the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}_{xx} & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})^{2} \\\\  & =(13-11)^{2}+(10-11)^{2}+(9-11)^{2}+(12-11)^{2} \\\\  & =4+1+4+1 \\\\  & =10 \\\\ {S}_{yy} & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({y}_{i}-\\overset{―}{y})^{2} \\\\  & =(-1-2)^{2}+(3-2)^{2}+(2-2)^{2}+(4-2)^{2} \\\\  & =9+1+0+4 \\\\  & =14\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we use the formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\rho (x,y) & =\\frac{(-5)}{\\sqrt{10}\\cdot \\sqrt{14}} \\\\  & ≈-\\frac{5}{3.162\\,278\\cdot 3.741\\,657} \\\\  & ≈-0.42\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $2$ decimal places."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096927 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096727 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "76569338-1e2a-5b12-96ef-685d272343cd" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Correlation Coefficient Using Covariance and Standard Deviations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "541541a4-3c88-59be-827c-990dca5f7555" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\rho =0.9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546433} {:answer/id #uuid "67d3b00b-9f50-5960-b07a-cae9063abc98" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\rho =-0.9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546478} {:answer/id #uuid "8fd94725-af10-52d7-9a5c-cd87508f6f0c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\rho =-0.3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546563} {:answer/id #uuid "9b0d1952-3e35-5e57-89da-978bb1e65270" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\rho =-0.6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546598} {:answer/id #uuid "a66f2630-f01a-52e9-aef9-e91092612030" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\rho =1.8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546624}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9b0d1952-3e35-5e57-89da-978bb1e65270" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\rho =-0.6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546598} :answer-field/id #uuid "d47e0d00-6213-58d4-b4c4-0da7914c3883" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186093536}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "2811c370-5b30-5984-8372-c002bcffbf44" :question/math-academy-id "q-10271" :question/problem "Consider the data sets $x$ and $y$ such that $\\text{Cov}(x,y)=-6$ and their standard deviations are ${\\sigma }_{x}=2$ and ${\\sigma }_{y}=5.$ Compute the corresponding linear correlation coefficient." :question/requires-calculator true :question/worked-solution "The (population) linear correlation coefficient for data sets $x$ and $y$ is given by the following formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\rho (x,y) & =\\frac{\\text{Cov}(x,y)}{{\\sigma }_{x}\\cdot {\\sigma }_{y}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting our numbers, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\rho (x,y) & =\\frac{(-6)}{2\\cdot 5} \\\\  & =-0.6.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186546986 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096726 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e2f40eb3-6087-5c38-b7c6-e160ff925bc6" :knowledge-point/title "Interpreting Linear Correlation Coefficients in Context"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2510127e-62c0-531b-a090-2890bcaa6a0a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546331} {:answer/id #uuid "865ec0da-3664-5085-9332-c3cb465e50cd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546548} {:answer/id #uuid "bc99633b-e718-5646-9aa4-66a9e6cccde7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546673} {:answer/id #uuid "c1366e14-06ea-55b7-847a-fd6744ad17f0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546685} {:answer/id #uuid "eb6e17a1-8c08-51d0-8118-d358e5e1ea2a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546781}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "865ec0da-3664-5085-9332-c3cb465e50cd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546548} :answer-field/id #uuid "57bf012b-e985-55c0-8315-ed64f981c5f5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546864}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "2fb87a4a-c905-590a-aa16-a1b45daeaaf5" :question/math-academy-id "q-134358" :question/problem "The owner of a fast food stall wants to check the strength of the linear correlation between the following variables:\n\n- $x,$ the price per bag of onion rings, in dollars, and\n\n- $y,$ the number of bags of onion rings sold that day.\n\nYou are given that $\\text{Cov}(x,y)=-5,$ ${\\sigma }_{x}=1,$ and ${\\sigma }_{y}=6.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $\\rho ≈-0.83$\n\nII. There is a strong negative correlation between $x$ and $y.$\n\nIII. There is a weak negative correlation between $x$ and $y.$\n\n*Hint: $|\\rho |>0.7$ indicates a strong correlation, and $0.3\\le |\\rho |\\le 0.7$ indicates a weak correlation.*" :question/worked-solution "The (population) linear correlation coefficient for data sets $x$ and $y$ is given by the following formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\rho (x,y) & =\\frac{\\text{Cov}(x,y)}{{\\sigma }_{x}\\cdot {\\sigma }_{y}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn addition, we have the following rule of thumb:\n\n- if $|\\rho |>0.7,$ the correlation is *strong*\n\n- if $0.3\\le |\\rho |\\le 0.7,$ the correlation is *weak*\n\n- if $|\\rho |<0.3,$ then the correlation is *negligible*\n\nLet's now examine our statements in turn:\n\n- Statement I is true. Calculating the correlation coefficient, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\rho (x,y) & =\\frac{-5}{1\\cdot 6} \\\\  & =\\frac{-5}{6} \\\\  & ≈-0.83.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true, while statement III is false. Since $|\\rho |>0.7,$ the correlation is strong. Also, since $\\rho $ is negative, the correlation is negative.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186547003 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096726 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e2f40eb3-6087-5c38-b7c6-e160ff925bc6" :knowledge-point/title "Interpreting Linear Correlation Coefficients in Context"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3c12122c-52da-5842-87cf-be90d71575fd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546382} {:answer/id #uuid "66ce4358-2394-58bb-83f1-93a7b3885b55" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546476} {:answer/id #uuid "76554064-dbbd-545c-8ea3-3674d21496d4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546515} {:answer/id #uuid "e6f10b2f-4613-510b-bee5-7d4497041a13" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546766} {:answer/id #uuid "fd88a7c7-483b-5be3-8a8f-267e89a3b634" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546823}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3c12122c-52da-5842-87cf-be90d71575fd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546382} :answer-field/id #uuid "6b543cab-3aef-506a-9921-1c34476f23c7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546872}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "5218551b-c171-5334-a3fb-989e7c3fce63" :question/math-academy-id "q-134359" :question/problem "Olivia plays soccer. She wants to check the strength of the linear correlation between the following variables:\n\n- $x,$ the ages of Olivia's teammates, in years, and\n\n- $y,$ their weights, in kilograms.\n\nYou are given that $\\text{Cov}(x,y)=8,$ ${\\sigma }_{x}=2,$ and ${\\sigma }_{y}=5.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $\\rho =0.8$\n\nII. There is a strong positive correlation between $x$ and $y.$\n\nIII. There is a weak positive correlation between $x$ and $y.$\n\n*Hint: $|\\rho |>0.7$ indicates a strong correlation, and $0.3\\le |\\rho |\\le 0.7$ indicates a weak correlation.*" :question/worked-solution "The (population) linear correlation coefficient for data sets $x$ and $y$ is given by the following formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\rho (x,y) & =\\frac{\\text{Cov}(x,y)}{{\\sigma }_{x}\\cdot {\\sigma }_{y}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn addition, we have the following rule of thumb:\n\n- if $|\\rho |>0.7,$ the correlation is *strong*\n\n- if $0.3\\le |\\rho |\\le 0.7,$ the correlation is *weak*\n\n- if $|\\rho |<0.3,$ then the correlation is *negligible*\n\nLet's now examine our statements in turn:\n\n- Statement I is true. Calculating the correlation coefficient, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\rho (x,y) & =\\frac{8}{2\\cdot 5} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{5} \\\\  & =0.8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true, while statement III is false. Since $|\\rho |>0.7,$ the correlation is strong. Also, since $\\rho $ is positive, the correlation is positive.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \" I and II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186547043 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096726 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e2f40eb3-6087-5c38-b7c6-e160ff925bc6" :knowledge-point/title "Interpreting Linear Correlation Coefficients in Context"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0e4922d4-abcb-5bce-bb9e-6a959e1e184f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546278} {:answer/id #uuid "3f88fad8-2eb7-5eee-8188-957d08a6face" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546394} {:answer/id #uuid "d4323d0d-2cc5-57e1-be5e-c2b662bb51ef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546720} {:answer/id #uuid "d4b5e441-37d1-5471-8ee9-a00f8c84b34d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546723} {:answer/id #uuid "ea4a039a-0a14-5e0c-b1d8-3bb4461468a6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546778}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d4323d0d-2cc5-57e1-be5e-c2b662bb51ef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546720} :answer-field/id #uuid "35bce278-6eb6-589e-8ac0-3a4ab76c202d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546848}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "b1bd60db-c84d-5039-99c1-be6e7b055360" :question/math-academy-id "q-134360" :question/problem "Mark collected information about the weather in his city every day during a particular year. He wants to check the strength of the linear correlation between the following variables:\n\n- $x,$ the temperature, in Celsius degrees, and\n\n- $y,$ the relative humidity, expressed as a percentage.\n\nYou are given that $\\text{Cov}(x,y)=75,$ ${\\sigma }_{x}=14,$ and ${\\sigma }_{y}=15.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $\\rho ≈0.44$\n\nII. There is a strong positive correlation between $x$ and $y.$\n\nIII. There is a weak positive correlation between $x$ and $y.$\n\n*Hint: $|\\rho |>0.7$ indicates a strong correlation, and $0.3\\le |\\rho |\\le 0.7$ indicates a weak correlation.*" :question/worked-solution "The (population) linear correlation coefficient for data sets $x$ and $y$ is given by the following formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\rho (x,y) & =\\frac{\\text{Cov}(x,y)}{{\\sigma }_{x}\\cdot {\\sigma }_{y}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn addition, we have the following rule of thumb:\n\n- if $|\\rho |>0.7,$ the correlation is *strong*\n\n- if $0.3\\le |\\rho |\\le 0.7,$ the correlation is *weak*\n\n- if $|\\rho |<0.3,$ then the correlation is *negligible*\n\nLet's now examine our statements in turn:\n\n- Statement I is false. Calculating the correlation coefficient, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\rho (x,y) & =\\frac{75}{14\\cdot 15} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{14} \\\\  & ≈0.36.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false, while statement III is true. Since $0.3\\le |\\rho |\\le 0.7,$ the correlation is weak. Also, since $\\rho $ is positive, the correlation is positive.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186546994 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096727 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "76569338-1e2a-5b12-96ef-685d272343cd" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Correlation Coefficient Using Covariance and Standard Deviations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "212c41fd-4470-5d76-bc0d-262704e89a01" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\rho ≈-0.14" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546322} {:answer/id #uuid "22a88291-8895-5c17-ad16-15fb539adad1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\rho ≈0.65" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546326} {:answer/id #uuid "6c1367ec-3a9c-5b70-bf15-6d39833f3db2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\rho ≈0.27" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546490} {:answer/id #uuid "a62a9b73-dfae-5534-ac70-28f789c94b6b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\rho ≈-0.67" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546622} {:answer/id #uuid "bf7f4529-994b-5d6e-878c-b64e9edd5eb3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\rho ≈-0.33" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546680}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a62a9b73-dfae-5534-ac70-28f789c94b6b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\rho ≈-0.67" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546622} :answer-field/id #uuid "a140b1f3-d505-561f-9a37-87c012828f40" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546899}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "3fd5d5a2-1dfb-5532-b6de-c6fae7626334" :question/math-academy-id "q-134393" :question/problem "Consider the data sets $x$ and $y$ such that $\\text{Cov}(x,y)=-8$ and their standard deviations are ${\\sigma }_{x}=3$ and ${\\sigma }_{y}=4.$ Compute the corresponding linear correlation coefficient. Round your answer to $2$ decimal places." :question/worked-solution "The (population) linear correlation coefficient for data sets $x$ and $y$ is given by the following formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\rho (x,y) & =\\frac{\\text{Cov}(x,y)}{{\\sigma }_{x}\\cdot {\\sigma }_{y}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting our numbers, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\rho (x,y) & =\\frac{(-8)}{3\\cdot 4} \\\\  & ≈-0.67\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $2$ decimal places."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186546973 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096727 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "76569338-1e2a-5b12-96ef-685d272343cd" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Correlation Coefficient Using Covariance and Standard Deviations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1d7a2874-ce4b-5a4f-9781-6331b1eaab57" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\rho ≈0.13" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546316} {:answer/id #uuid "691f136c-410a-5f67-9c5f-2b3c09abc005" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\rho ≈0.67" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546480} {:answer/id #uuid "886cd3e2-59d5-5526-996e-275da9c4a554" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\rho ≈0.75" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546551} {:answer/id #uuid "a1b95fe2-46b9-516a-b272-d4e2ccbf9a80" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\rho ≈0.50" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546611} {:answer/id #uuid "e0b7be75-5ea1-52c2-9681-222df8a72703" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\rho ≈0.42" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546752}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a1b95fe2-46b9-516a-b272-d4e2ccbf9a80" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\rho ≈0.50" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546611} :answer-field/id #uuid "02b295dd-b963-57b2-a571-57614a7d6b62" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546831}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "20c0048f-c0fd-59d4-abe6-fec1d5f41697" :question/math-academy-id "q-134398" :question/problem "Consider the following data sets:\n\n$$\nx=\\{23,\\,21,\\,25\\},\\,y=\\{10,\\,8,\\,9\\}\n$$\n\nYou're given that $\\overset{―}{x}=23,$ $\\overset{―}{y}=9,$ ${\\sigma }_{x}≈1.633,$ and ${\\sigma }_{y}≈0.816.$ Calculate the corresponding linear correlation coefficient. Round your answer to $2$ decimal places." :question/worked-solution "The (population) linear correlation coefficient of the data sets $x=\\{{x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}\\}$ and $y=\\{{y}_{1},{y}_{2},…,{y}_{n}\\}$ is given by \n\n$$\n\\rho (x,y)=\\frac{\\text{Cov}(x,y)}{{\\sigma }_{x}\\cdot {\\sigma }_{y}}\n$$\n\nwhere $\\text{Cov}(x,y)$ is the covariance, and ${\\sigma }_{x}$ and ${\\sigma }_{y}$ are the standard deviations of the data sets.\n\nFirst, we need to compute the covariance:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Cov}(x,y) & =\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})({y}_{i}-\\overset{―}{y}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}[(23-23)(10-9)+(21-23)(8-9)+(25-23)(9-9)] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}[(0)(1)+(-2)(-1)+(2)(0)] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot 2 \\\\  & ≈0.667\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we use the formula. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\rho (x,y) & =\\frac{\\text{Cov}(x,y)}{{\\sigma }_{x}\\cdot {\\sigma }_{y}} \\\\  & ≈\\frac{0.667}{1.633\\cdot 0.816} \\\\  & ≈0.50\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $2$ decimal places."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186546968 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096727 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "76569338-1e2a-5b12-96ef-685d272343cd" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Correlation Coefficient Using Covariance and Standard Deviations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7dd51322-e8fb-5da6-8b86-a44dc4d96653" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\rho ≈0.98" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546529} {:answer/id #uuid "8ff63289-e195-5ca0-9c0f-60c6d613464e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\rho ≈0.84" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546565} {:answer/id #uuid "d6a0b584-89d2-56d7-bceb-37505c98ad5a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\rho ≈0.54" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546727} {:answer/id #uuid "d9a62bcd-5c05-525d-941e-1f20d80183eb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\rho ≈0.33" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546731} {:answer/id #uuid "e9014057-f209-5b30-93b9-61fecfa103aa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\rho ≈0.67" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546772}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7dd51322-e8fb-5da6-8b86-a44dc4d96653" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\rho ≈0.98" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546529} :answer-field/id #uuid "ffd11057-b685-55c9-893f-a00c1da187d1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546957}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "19d5c66d-e74b-5c3e-98da-49c6a7aecbf3" :question/math-academy-id "q-134399" :question/problem "Consider the following data sets:\n\n$$\nx=\\{2,\\,1,\\,-1\\},\\,y=\\{15,\\,12,\\,9\\}\n$$\n\nYou're given that $\\overset{―}{x}=\\frac{2}{3},$ $\\overset{―}{y}=12,$ ${\\sigma }_{x}≈1.247,$ and ${\\sigma }_{y}≈2.449.$ Calculate the corresponding linear correlation coefficient. Round your answer to $2$ decimal places." :question/worked-solution "The (population) linear correlation coefficient of the data sets $x=\\{{x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}\\}$ and $y=\\{{y}_{1},{y}_{2},…,{y}_{n}\\}$ is given by \n\n$$\n\\rho (x,y)=\\frac{\\text{Cov}(x,y)}{{\\sigma }_{x}\\cdot {\\sigma }_{y}}\n$$\n\nwhere $\\text{Cov}(x,y)$ is the covariance, and ${\\sigma }_{x}$ and ${\\sigma }_{y}$ are the standard deviations of the data sets.\n\nFirst, we need to compute the covariance:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Cov}(x,y) & =\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})({y}_{i}-\\overset{―}{y}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}[(2-\\frac{2}{3})(15-12)+(1-\\frac{2}{3})(12-12)+(-1-\\frac{2}{3})(9-12)] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}[(\\frac{4}{3})(3)+(\\frac{1}{3})(0)+(-\\frac{5}{3})(-3)] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}[4+0+5] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot 9 \\\\  & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we use the formula. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\rho (x,y) & =\\frac{\\text{Cov}(x,y)}{{\\sigma }_{x}\\cdot {\\sigma }_{y}} \\\\  & ≈\\frac{3}{1.247\\cdot 2.449} \\\\  & ≈0.98\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $2$ decimal places."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186546979 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096727 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "76569338-1e2a-5b12-96ef-685d272343cd" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Correlation Coefficient Using Covariance and Standard Deviations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0143626d-ccad-5fbc-98cc-c8c52843f599" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\rho ≈-0.42" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546255} {:answer/id #uuid "39c75b7f-b404-5c7b-9bbc-7a77b487b96b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\rho ≈0.04" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546372} {:answer/id #uuid "48d92832-ceb8-58b7-89f1-c2c08d4d835c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\rho ≈-0.19" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546413} {:answer/id #uuid "902c78fa-80db-5420-8738-b4abd82215e3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\rho ≈0.78" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546566} {:answer/id #uuid "938a74ae-a07e-5ee4-b10b-4a8d800343d9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\rho ≈-0.76" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546577}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "48d92832-ceb8-58b7-89f1-c2c08d4d835c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\rho ≈-0.19" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546413} :answer-field/id #uuid "38a6d22c-12e8-52dc-9c96-83418688b56c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546850}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "24874dea-ae30-58b3-9c5c-bcedba1ee115" :question/math-academy-id "q-134400" :question/problem "Consider the following data sets:\n\n$$\nx=\\{7,\\,2,\\,12\\},\\,y=\\{-1,\\,9,\\,7\\}\n$$\n\nYou're given that $\\overset{―}{x}=7,$ $\\overset{―}{y}=5,$ ${\\sigma }_{x}≈4.082,$ and ${\\sigma }_{y}≈4.320.$ Calculate the corresponding linear correlation coefficient. Round your answer to $2$ decimal places." :question/worked-solution "The (population) linear correlation coefficient of the data sets $x=\\{{x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}\\}$ and $y=\\{{y}_{1},{y}_{2},…,{y}_{n}\\}$ is given by \n\n$$\n\\rho (x,y)=\\frac{\\text{Cov}(x,y)}{{\\sigma }_{x}\\cdot {\\sigma }_{y}}\n$$\n\nwhere $\\text{Cov}(x,y)$ is the covariance, and ${\\sigma }_{x}$ and ${\\sigma }_{y}$ are the standard deviations of the data sets.\n\nFirst, we need to compute the covariance:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Cov}(x,y) & =\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})({y}_{i}-\\overset{―}{y}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}[(7-7)(-1-5)+(2-7)(9-5)+(12-7)(7-5)] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}[(0)(-6)+(-5)(4)+(5)(2)] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}[0-20+10] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot (-10) \\\\  & ≈-3.333\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we use the formula. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\rho (x,y) & =\\frac{\\text{Cov}(x,y)}{{\\sigma }_{x}\\cdot {\\sigma }_{y}} \\\\  & ≈\\frac{(-3.333)}{4.082\\cdot 4.320} \\\\  & ≈-0.19\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $2$ decimal places."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186547041 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096726 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e2f40eb3-6087-5c38-b7c6-e160ff925bc6" :knowledge-point/title "Interpreting Linear Correlation Coefficients in Context"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0b9b6447-5e81-5ee1-b93e-f349481ecf21" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546270} {:answer/id #uuid "1f0401d4-997c-5e0e-a92c-bddf630c789e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546318} {:answer/id #uuid "3e641f3d-eaf3-5390-a911-b775f5999ebc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546391} {:answer/id #uuid "ce4b8373-460f-5dbf-8df7-974dfa84cfd5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546711} {:answer/id #uuid "fb05b1cb-d912-53a1-83e4-41068da91e3c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546811}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ce4b8373-460f-5dbf-8df7-974dfa84cfd5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546711} :answer-field/id #uuid "b0ac2d8e-1510-54ca-9445-4aec50f70251" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546918}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "aab6da21-f97b-5ce7-b157-a7c2c5069279" :question/math-academy-id "q-134404" :question/problem "An ergonomist investigates $4$ factories. He wants to check the strength of the linear correlation between $x,$ the money spent by factory owners on safety training courses, and $y,$ the number of accidents per year in the factories. The data is shown below.\n\n| **Money spent (hundreds of $$$)** | **$10$** | **$15$** | **$12$** | **$23$** |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| **Number of accidents** | $3$ | $6$ | $5$ | $2$ |\n\nYou are given that the means are $\\overset{―}{x}=15$ and $\\overset{―}{y}=4.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $\\rho ≈-0.45$\n\nII. There is a strong negative correlation between $x$ and $y.$\n\nIII. There is a weak negative correlation between $x$ and $y.$\n\nHint: $|\\rho |>0.7$ indicates a strong correlation, and $0.3\\le |\\rho |\\le 0.7$ indicates a weak correlation." :question/worked-solution "The linear correlation coefficient $\\rho $ of the data sets $x=\\{{x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}\\}$ and $y=\\{{y}_{1},{y}_{2},…,{y}_{n}\\}$ is given by \n\n$$\n\\rho (x,y)=\\frac{\\text{Cov}(x,y)}{{\\sigma }_{x}\\cdot {\\sigma }_{y}}=\\frac{{S}_{xy}}{\\sqrt{{S}_{xx}\\cdot {S}_{yy}}}.\n$$\n\nIn addition, we have the following rule of thumb:\n\n- if $|\\rho |>0.7,$ the correlation is *strong*\n\n- if $0.3\\le |\\rho |\\le 0.7,$ the correlation is *weak*\n\n- if $|\\rho |<0.3,$ then the correlation is *negligible*\n\nLet's now examine our statements in turn:\n\n- Statement I is true. To calculate $\\rho ,$ we first compute the sum in the numerator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}_{xy} & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})({y}_{i}-\\overset{―}{y}) \\\\  & =(10-15)(3-4)+(15-15)(6-4) \\\\  & +(12-15)(5-4)+(23-15)(2-4) \\\\  & =(-5)(-1)+(0)(2)+(-3)(1)+(8)(-2) \\\\  & =5+0-3-16 \\\\  & =-14\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSecond, we compute the sums in the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}_{xx} & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})^{2} \\\\  & =(10-15)^{2}+(15-15)^{2}+(12-15)^{2}+(23-15)^{2} \\\\  & =25+0+9+64 \\\\  & =98 \\\\ {S}_{yy} & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({y}_{i}-\\overset{―}{y})^{2} \\\\  & =(3-4)^{2}+(6-4)^{2}+(5-4)^{2}+(2-4)^{2} \\\\  & =1+4+1+4 \\\\  & =10\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we use the formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\rho (x,y) & =\\frac{-14}{\\sqrt{98}\\cdot \\sqrt{10}} \\\\  & ≈-\\frac{14}{9.899\\,495\\cdot 3.162\\,278} \\\\  & ≈-0.45,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $2$ decimal places.\n\n- Statement II is false while statement III is true. Since $0.3\\le |\\rho |\\le 0.7,$ the correlation is weak. Also, since $\\rho $ is negative, the correlation is negative.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186546990 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096726 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e2f40eb3-6087-5c38-b7c6-e160ff925bc6" :knowledge-point/title "Interpreting Linear Correlation Coefficients in Context"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4559fe1f-5cc5-50c5-a69c-e95fdd78cc94" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546405} {:answer/id #uuid "7cad5610-2a07-5f0c-ae88-c79dd7b2917f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546527} {:answer/id #uuid "b511dbe1-91cf-5909-8045-8b78de98ae64" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546656} {:answer/id #uuid "c438f6aa-5a1b-51db-ad5a-70f469878ed2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546689} {:answer/id #uuid "f514a699-7e5c-5a31-9eaa-07f00c4b3a09" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546803}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4559fe1f-5cc5-50c5-a69c-e95fdd78cc94" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546405} :answer-field/id #uuid "bf65d2d2-724d-5cf1-bd32-eea27646ffc4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546927}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "382df20d-c998-524f-8214-a4f7f23f788c" :question/math-academy-id "q-134412" :question/problem "A teacher of a group of $4$ students wants to check the strength of the linear correlation between $x,$ the number of hours the students had slept the night before an exam, and $y,$ the student's score in the exam. The data is shown below. \n\n| **Number of hours** | $2$ | $4$ | $6$ | $8$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| **Score ** | $56$ | $68$ | $81$ | $79$ |\n\nYou are given that the means are $\\overset{―}{x}=5$ and $\\overset{―}{y}=71.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $\\rho ≈0.81$\n\nII. There is a strong positive correlation between $x$ and $y.$\n\nIII. There is a weak positive correlation between $x$ and $y.$\n\n*Hint: $|\\rho |>0.7$ indicates a strong correlation, and $0.3\\le |\\rho |\\le 0.7$ indicates a weak correlation.*" :question/worked-solution "The linear correlation coefficient $\\rho $ of the data sets $x=\\{{x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}\\}$ and $y=\\{{y}_{1},{y}_{2},…,{y}_{n}\\}$ is given by \n\n$$\n\\rho (x,y)=\\frac{\\text{Cov}(x,y)}{{\\sigma }_{x}\\cdot {\\sigma }_{y}}=\\frac{{S}_{xy}}{\\sqrt{{S}_{xx}\\cdot {S}_{yy}}}.\n$$\n\nIn addition, we have the following rule of thumb:\n\n- if $|\\rho |>0.7,$ the correlation is *strong*\n\n- if $0.3\\le |\\rho |\\le 0.7,$ the correlation is *weak*\n\n- if $|\\rho |<0.3,$ then the correlation is *negligible*\n\nLet's now examine our statements in turn:\n\n- Statement I is false. To calculate $\\rho ,$ we first compute the sum in the numerator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}_{xy} & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})({y}_{i}-\\overset{―}{y}) \\\\  & =(2-5)(56-71)+(4-5)(68-71) \\\\  & +(6-5)(81-71)+(8-5)(79-71) \\\\  & =(-3)(-15)+(-1)(-3)+(1)(10)+(3)(8) \\\\  & =45+3+10+24 \\\\  & =82\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSecond, we compute the sums in the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}_{xx} & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})^{2} \\\\  & =(2-5)^{2}+(4-5)^{2}+(6-5)^{2}+(8-5)^{2} \\\\  & =9+1+1+9 \\\\  & =20 \\\\ {S}_{yy} & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({y}_{i}-\\overset{―}{y})^{2} \\\\  & =(56-71)^{2}+(68-71)^{2}+(81-71)^{2}+(79-71)^{2} \\\\  & =225+9+100+64 \\\\  & =398\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we use the formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\rho (x,y) & =\\frac{82}{\\sqrt{20}\\cdot \\sqrt{398}} \\\\  & ≈\\frac{82}{4.472\\,136\\cdot 19.949\\,937} \\\\  & ≈0.92,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true, while statement III is false. Since $|\\rho |>0.7,$ the correlation is strong. Also, since $\\rho $ is positive, the correlation is positive.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186546960 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096786 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "dd2caf12-80c2-553a-9737-64151565cf86" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Linear Correlation Coefficient Using the Expanded Formula"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "18738bd6-2a2c-5c16-b8aa-d191f9e6c4a6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\rho ≈-0.35" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546304} {:answer/id #uuid "930b877c-908d-58d8-96e5-13939f72c263" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\rho ≈-0.29" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546575} {:answer/id #uuid "a8e544ce-5135-51f5-a821-11275c4e94f2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\rho ≈-0.31" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546628} {:answer/id #uuid "d62f92b2-c4e4-5b9f-af3a-872bbac9845e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\rho ≈0.31" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546726} {:answer/id #uuid "fc8f191f-a0ac-5346-adf3-fb2c94908df1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\rho ≈0.35" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546817}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "18738bd6-2a2c-5c16-b8aa-d191f9e6c4a6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\rho ≈-0.35" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546304} :answer-field/id #uuid "b41d670d-8f5a-506f-97ae-24a1d59a59cb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546920}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "02585424-6726-555e-b4dc-cd93537b87a1" :question/math-academy-id "q-134448" :question/problem "The data sets $x$ and $y$ have the following properties:\n\n$$\n{S}_{xx}=89,\\,{S}_{xy}=-69,\\,{S}_{yy}=426\n$$\n\nCalculate the corresponding linear correlation coefficient. Round your answer to $2$ decimal places." :question/worked-solution "Recall the definitions of ${S}_{xx},$ ${S}_{xy},$ and ${S}_{yy}$ for the data sets $x=\\{{x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}\\}$ and $y=\\{{y}_{1},{y}_{2},…,{y}_{n}\\}:$\n\n$$\n{S}_{xx}={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})^{2},\\,{S}_{xy}={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})({y}_{i}-\\overset{―}{y}),\\,{S}_{yy}={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({y}_{i}-\\overset{―}{y})^{2}\n$$\n\nThe linear correlation coefficient $\\rho $ of the data sets $x$ and $y$ is given by \n\n$$\n\\rho (x,y)=\\frac{\\text{Cov}(x,y)}{{\\sigma }_{x}\\cdot {\\sigma }_{y}}=\\frac{{S}_{xy}}{\\sqrt{{S}_{xx}\\cdot {S}_{yy}}}.\n$$\n\nUsing the formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\rho (x,y) & =\\frac{(-69)}{\\sqrt{89}\\cdot \\sqrt{426}} \\\\  & ≈-\\frac{69}{9.433\\,981\\cdot 20.639\\,767} \\\\  & ≈-0.35\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $2$ decimal places."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186546993 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096786 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "dd2caf12-80c2-553a-9737-64151565cf86" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Linear Correlation Coefficient Using the Expanded Formula"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "39e0e51f-31ac-5155-8515-0e388c62fff3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\rho ≈-0.78" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546373} {:answer/id #uuid "5cce0b31-4bcb-572e-b042-e52b018d39b9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\rho ≈0.78" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546452} {:answer/id #uuid "8a800801-9386-5b81-a40b-00038098eb6c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\rho ≈-0.69" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546556} {:answer/id #uuid "bd17caa6-972d-5ccd-b882-e9178d4ee21f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\rho ≈-0.57" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546676} {:answer/id #uuid "c15c2293-64f4-5914-a1bd-d8219b7ea7b7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\rho ≈0.69" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546686}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "39e0e51f-31ac-5155-8515-0e388c62fff3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\rho ≈-0.78" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546373} :answer-field/id #uuid "8529b028-3069-5aca-862e-01bc09be8701" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546888}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "3e0b60bf-7e6e-5590-be27-0060180cfb46" :question/math-academy-id "q-134449" :question/problem "The data sets $x$ and $y$ have the following properties:\n\n$$\n{S}_{xx}=41,\\,{S}_{xy}=-39,\\,{S}_{yy}=61\n$$\n\nCalculate the corresponding linear correlation coefficient. Round your answer to $2$ decimal places." :question/worked-solution "Recall the definitions of ${S}_{xx},$ ${S}_{xy},$ and ${S}_{yy}$ for the data sets $x=\\{{x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}\\}$ and $y=\\{{y}_{1},{y}_{2},…,{y}_{n}\\}:$\n\n$$\n{S}_{xx}={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})^{2},\\,{S}_{xy}={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})({y}_{i}-\\overset{―}{y}),\\,{S}_{yy}={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({y}_{i}-\\overset{―}{y})^{2}\n$$\n\nThe linear correlation coefficient $\\rho $ of the data sets $x$ and $y$ is given by \n\n$$\n\\rho (x,y)=\\frac{\\text{Cov}(x,y)}{{\\sigma }_{x}\\cdot {\\sigma }_{y}}=\\frac{{S}_{xy}}{\\sqrt{{S}_{xx}\\cdot {S}_{yy}}}.\n$$\n\nUsing the formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\rho (x,y) & =\\frac{(-39)}{\\sqrt{41}\\cdot \\sqrt{61}} \\\\  & ≈-\\frac{39}{6.403\\,124\\cdot 7.810\\,250} \\\\  & ≈-0.78\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $2$ decimal places."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186547068 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096786 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "dd2caf12-80c2-553a-9737-64151565cf86" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Linear Correlation Coefficient Using the Expanded Formula"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1d5ed8ea-0d2f-598e-bbee-fa0331eb19ff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\rho ≈0.03" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546314} {:answer/id #uuid "2b275c6e-b63d-588b-908a-9be2fcc988a8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\rho ≈0.01" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546344} {:answer/id #uuid "d56f4afa-8ea0-5033-9570-ec6824ffd318" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\rho ≈0.07" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546724} {:answer/id #uuid "d9245115-c551-5f31-bb38-69e994a7f681" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\rho ≈0.15" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546729} {:answer/id #uuid "fb713398-5c7b-526f-ac30-7b41aa74c8a1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\rho ≈0.21" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546812}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2b275c6e-b63d-588b-908a-9be2fcc988a8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\rho ≈0.01" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546344} :answer-field/id #uuid "fbae2f81-ee76-564e-9a95-3a28d9bfac64" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546954}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e77a74f0-0303-531d-b1fe-933e8eb337ca" :question/math-academy-id "q-134450" :question/problem "The data sets $x$ and $y$ have the following properties:\n\n$$\n{S}_{xx}=186,\\,{S}_{xy}=2,\\,{S}_{yy}=96\n$$\n\nCalculate the corresponding linear correlation coefficient. Round your answer to $2$ decimal places." :question/worked-solution "Recall the definitions of ${S}_{xx},$ ${S}_{xy},$ and ${S}_{yy}$ for the data sets $x=\\{{x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}\\}$ and $y=\\{{y}_{1},{y}_{2},…,{y}_{n}\\}:$\n\n$$\n{S}_{xx}={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})^{2},\\,{S}_{xy}={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})({y}_{i}-\\overset{―}{y}),\\,{S}_{yy}={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({y}_{i}-\\overset{―}{y})^{2}\n$$\n\nThe linear correlation coefficient $\\rho $ of the data sets $x$ and $y$ is given by \n\n$$\n\\rho (x,y)=\\frac{\\text{Cov}(x,y)}{{\\sigma }_{x}\\cdot {\\sigma }_{y}}=\\frac{{S}_{xy}}{\\sqrt{{S}_{xx}\\cdot {S}_{yy}}}.\n$$\n\nUsing the formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\rho (x,y) & =\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{186}\\cdot \\sqrt{96}} \\\\  & ≈\\frac{2}{13.638\\,182\\cdot 9.797\\,959} \\\\  & ≈0.01\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $2$ decimal places."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186547035 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096786 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "dd2caf12-80c2-553a-9737-64151565cf86" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Linear Correlation Coefficient Using the Expanded Formula"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3aa92092-4369-5ff2-8cce-7258d56ad264" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\rho =-0.25" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546379} {:answer/id #uuid "4dfd1c2e-ea6f-5434-a4d9-c0b132425dc5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\rho =-0.2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546423} {:answer/id #uuid "74b331b0-c98a-5a41-94f1-cfd562c37a49" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\rho =0.25" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546510} {:answer/id #uuid "a0660bf7-cc1e-50a6-b15e-2bc59fd44ea8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\rho =0.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546608} {:answer/id #uuid "c6783e59-5110-5183-8ab7-9fe21fa826d3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\rho =-0.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546693}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c6783e59-5110-5183-8ab7-9fe21fa826d3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\rho =-0.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546693} :answer-field/id #uuid "bce4a32c-46b5-57a1-9ab0-97e8c5d0e836" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546925}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "999285af-eeb2-534a-9bcd-a9a462f931b2" :question/math-academy-id "q-134454" :question/problem "Consider the following data sets:\n\n$$\nx=\\{5,\\,8,\\,2\\},\\,y=\\{-1,\\,2,\\,5\\}\n$$\n\nYou are given that $\\overset{―}{x}=5$ and $\\overset{―}{y}=2.$ Calculate the corresponding linear correlation coefficient." :question/worked-solution "The linear correlation coefficient $\\rho $ of the data sets $x=\\{{x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}\\}$ and $y=\\{{y}_{1},{y}_{2},…,{y}_{n}\\}$ is given by \n\n$$\n\\rho (x,y)=\\frac{\\text{Cov}(x,y)}{{\\sigma }_{x}\\cdot {\\sigma }_{y}}=\\frac{{S}_{xy}}{\\sqrt{{S}_{xx}\\cdot {S}_{yy}}}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute the sum in the numerator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}_{xy} & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})({y}_{i}-\\overset{―}{y}) \\\\  & =(5-5)(-1-2)+(8-5)(2-2)+(2-5)(5-2) \\\\  & =(0)(-3)+(3)(0)+(-3)(3) \\\\  & =0+0-9 \\\\  & =-9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSecond, we compute the sums in the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}_{xx} & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})^{2} \\\\  & =(5-5)^{2}+(8-5)^{2}+(2-5)^{2} \\\\  & =0+9+9 \\\\  & =18 \\\\ {S}_{yy} & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({y}_{i}-\\overset{―}{y})^{2} \\\\  & =(-1-2)^{2}+(2-2)^{2}+(5-2)^{2} \\\\  & =9+0+9 \\\\  & =18\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we use the formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\rho (x,y) & =\\frac{(-9)}{\\sqrt{18}\\cdot \\sqrt{18}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{-9}{3\\sqrt{2}\\cdot 3\\sqrt{2}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =-0.5\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186547078 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096786 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "dd2caf12-80c2-553a-9737-64151565cf86" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Linear Correlation Coefficient Using the Expanded Formula"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0c70e193-480f-5fd4-a75e-269757734d2b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\rho ≈-0.53" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546272} {:answer/id #uuid "15197980-ac5e-5a3b-abce-35c110615bc3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\rho ≈0.51" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546296} {:answer/id #uuid "4ff9aec8-d155-5665-988f-5d5fbe50f4dd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\rho ≈-0.44" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546426} {:answer/id #uuid "70f793c9-dbdd-5292-816a-30e294057003" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\rho ≈0.46" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546500} {:answer/id #uuid "85258801-bb79-5805-a720-da6148b5de85" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\rho ≈0.42" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546544}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "85258801-bb79-5805-a720-da6148b5de85" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\rho ≈0.42" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546544} :answer-field/id #uuid "52784d35-7cc8-5193-a5b2-29099f33cdec" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546863}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f535de3d-b5b2-5621-843d-e4b9ec9ed404" :question/math-academy-id "q-134455" :question/problem "Consider the following data sets:\n\n$$\nx=\\{13,\\,4,\\,10\\},\\,y=\\{0,\\,-3,\\,9\\}\n$$\n\nYou are given that $\\overset{―}{x}=9$ and $\\overset{―}{y}=2.$ Calculate the corresponding linear correlation coefficient. Round your answer to $2$ decimal places." :question/worked-solution "The linear correlation coefficient $\\rho $ of the data sets $x=\\{{x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}\\}$ and $y=\\{{y}_{1},{y}_{2},…,{y}_{n}\\}$ is given by \n\n$$\n\\rho (x,y)=\\frac{\\text{Cov}(x,y)}{{\\sigma }_{x}\\cdot {\\sigma }_{y}}=\\frac{{S}_{xy}}{\\sqrt{{S}_{xx}\\cdot {S}_{yy}}}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute the sum in the numerator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}_{xy} & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})({y}_{i}-\\overset{―}{y}) \\\\  & =(13-9)(0-2)+(4-9)(-3-2)+(10-9)(9-2) \\\\  & =(4)(-2)+(-5)(-5)+(1)(7) \\\\  & =-8+25+7 \\\\  & =24\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSecond, we compute the sums in the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}_{xx} & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})^{2} \\\\  & =(13-9)^{2}+(4-9)^{2}+(10-9)^{2} \\\\  & =16+25+1 \\\\  & =42 \\\\ {S}_{yy} & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({y}_{i}-\\overset{―}{y})^{2} \\\\  & =(0-2)^{2}+(-3-2)^{2}+(9-2)^{2} \\\\  & =4+25+49 \\\\  & =78\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we use the formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\rho (x,y) & =\\frac{24}{\\sqrt{42}\\cdot \\sqrt{78}} \\\\  & ≈\\frac{24}{6.480\\,741\\cdot 8.831\\,761} \\\\  & ≈0.42\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $2$ decimal places."}]}
